q 0 0 0 rg BT 12 TL/F3 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 250.555 800 Tm (FPSC MPT CSS-2022)Tj T* ET .5 w 55 792 m 540 792 l S BT 12.6 TL/F3 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 55 774 Tm (44. The rebel spies were charged with ______ and put on trial.)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 761 Tm (\(a\) Attrition)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 761 Tm (\(b\) Reiteration)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 747 Tm (\(c\) Sedition)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 747 Tm (\(d\) Perdition)Tj T* ET BT/F3 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 55 728 Tm (45. Zachary was doomed to a miserable life, for no matter how much he had, he always ______ the)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 715 Tm (possessions of others.)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 702 Tm (\(a\) Protracted)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 702 Tm (\(b\) Exalted)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 688 Tm (\(c\) Coveted)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 688 Tm (\(d\) Filibustered)Tj T* ET BT 13.2 TL/F3 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 239.288 665 Tm (Reading Comprehension)Tj T* ET BT 12 TL/F5 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 55 650 Tm (Glaciers consist of fallen snow that compresses over many years into large, thickened ice masses. Most of the world's)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 637.5 Tm (glacial ice is found in Antarctica and Greenland, but glaciers are found on nearly every continent, even Africa. Presently,)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 625 Tm (10% of the land area is covered with glaciers. Glacial ice often appears blue because the ice absorbs all other colors but)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 612.5 Tm (reflects blue. Almost 90% of an iceberg is below water, only about 10% shows above water. What makes glaciers unique)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 600 Tm (is their ability to move. Due to sheer mass, glaciers flow like very slow rivers. Some glaciers are as small as football)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 587.5 Tm (fields, whereas others grow to be over 100 kilometers long.)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 575 Tm (Within the past 750,000 years, scientists know that there have been eight Ice Age cycles, separated by warmer periods)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 562.5 Tm (called an interglacial period. Currently, the earth is nearing the end of an interglacial, meaning that another Ice Age is)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 550 Tm (due in a few thousand years. This is part of the normal climate variation cycle. Greenhouse warming may delay the onset)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 537.5 Tm (of another glacial era, but scientists still have many questions to answer about climate change. Although glaciers change)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 525 Tm (very slowly over long periods, they may provide important global climate change signals.)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 512.5 Tm (The girth of ice, combined with gravity's influence, causes glaciers to flow very slowly. Once a mass of compressed ice)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 500 Tm (reaches a critical thickness of about 18 meters thick, it becomes so heavy that it begins to deform and move. Ice may)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 487.5 Tm (flow down mountains and valleys, fan across plains, or spread out to sea. Movement along the underside of a glacier is)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 475 Tm (slower than movement at the top due to the friction created as it slides along the ground's surface.)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 462.5 Tm (Most glaciers are found in remote mountainous areas. However, some found near cities or towns present a danger to the)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 450 Tm (people living nearby. On land, lakes formed on top of a glacier during the melt season may cause floods. At the narrow)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 437.5 Tm (part of a valley glacier, ice falling from the glacier presents a hazard to hikers below. When the ice breaks off over the)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 425 Tm (ocean, an iceberg is formed.)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 412.5 Tm (Glaciers are a natural resource and contain 75% of the world's freshwater. People worldwide are trying to harness the)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 400 Tm (power of these frozen streams. Some towns rely on glacial melting from a nearby ice cap to provide drinking water.)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 387.5 Tm (Some farmers spread soil or ashes over the snow to promote melting, hoping that the melting will provide water to)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 375 Tm (irrigate crops in drought-stricken areas. Others have channeled meltwater from glaciers to their fields. Scientists and)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 362.5 Tm (engineers have worked together to tap into glacial resources, using electricity that has been generated in part by)Tj T* ET BT 1 0 0 1 55 350 Tm (damming glacial meltwater.)Tj T* ET BT 12.6 TL/F3 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 55 331.5 Tm (46. According to paragraph 4, what is a negative effect of living too close to a glacier?)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 318.5 Tm (\(a\) The mass of the glacier reaches a critical thickness)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 318.5 Tm (\(b\) About 10% of a glacier shows above water)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 304.5 Tm (\(c\) Spreading dark material over snow promotes melting)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 304.5 Tm (\(d\) Lakes formed on top of glaciers may cause floods)Tj T* ET BT/F3 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 55 285.5 Tm (47. The underlined word 'remote', as used in paragraph 4 of the passage, most nearly means:)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 272.5 Tm (\(a\) Isolated)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 272.5 Tm (\(b\) Nearby)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 258.5 Tm (\(c\) Slow travelling)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 258.5 Tm (\(d\) Difficult to see)Tj T* ET BT/F3 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 55 239.5 Tm (48. The passage explains that glaciers can be found where?)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 226.5 Tm (\(a\) Only on Antarctica)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 226.5 Tm (\(b\) Only Greenland and Alaska)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 212.5 Tm (\(c\) On nearly every continent)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 212.5 Tm (\(d\) On the north and south poles)Tj T* ET BT/F3 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 55 193.5 Tm (49. According to the passage, why does glacial ice often appear blue?)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 180.5 Tm (\(a\) Because it does not absorb the color blue)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 180.5 Tm (\(b\) Because it absorbs all other colors but reflects blue)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 166.5 Tm (\(c\) Because it does not absorb all other colors including blue)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 166.5 Tm (\(d\) Because it is blue in color)Tj T* ET BT/F3 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 55 147.5 Tm (50. After reading the passage, what can one conclude about glaciers?)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 134.5 Tm (\(a\) There will not be another Ice Age coming)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 134.5 Tm (\(b\) Glaciers have both negative and positive effects on human life)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 120.5 Tm (\(c\) Scientists have difficulty studying glaciers)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 120.5 Tm (\(d\) Scientists have minimal data on the formation of glaciers)Tj T* ET BT/F3 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 55 101.5 Tm (51. After reading the passage, what can one infer about glaciers?)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 88.5 Tm (\(a\) Further exploration is needed to tap the power of glacial ice in fuelling electric energy)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 88.5 Tm (\(b\) With variations in climate, glaciers shrink and expand)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 73 74.5 Tm (\(c\) Glaciers form in cold regions where the rate of snowfall is greater than the melting rate of snow)Tj T* ET BT/F5 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 315.5 74.5 Tm (\(d\) Glaciers are usually bordered at the sides by rock debris)Tj T* ET 1 1 1 rg BT 11.4 TL/F2 9.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 122.4483 8 Tm T* ET 0 0 0 rg BT 10.8 TL/F5 9 Tf 1 0 0 1 494.757 32 Tm T* ET Q q 1 1 1 rg 1 1 1 RG 0 0 595 72 re B Q q 1 1 1 rg 1 1 1 RG 119.4483 3.0835 356.10374 17.08148 re B Q
